WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.400
Ready to go!

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.880
President Lyndon Johnson's War on

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:11.620
Poverty had some important successes, but
it got at least one thing very wrong: Its

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:15.890
leaders used the language of civil rights
to talk about income and poverty.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:19.880
Financial assistance from the government
is not a right! It's not protected by the

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.470
Constitution like religious freedom or
freedom of speech. Using the language of

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:29.659
rights led to two big mistakes. First, it
told people in need that they had no

00:00:29.659 --> 00:00:33.320
role in improving their economic
situation; the government would take care

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.609
of everything. And second, it led to a
misguided effort to use Congress and

00:00:37.609 --> 00:00:43.070
federal courts to impose uniform
anti-poverty policies on every state. Helping

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.530
people struggling with poverty doesn't
work that way!

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:50.000
Individual responsibility plays a role.
It's not always sufficient – but it is

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.039
essential – and federal mandates often
mean decision makers are too far removed

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:58.969
from what is happening in cities and
states. Our anti-poverty programs work

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:03.829
best when we insist on some effort by
those seeking assistance, and we allow

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:06.500
states to devise the best approaches.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.600
What do you think about Lyndon Johnson's

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.380
War on Poverty? Let us know your comments.
Also, let us know what other topics you'd

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:21.020
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,
and be sure to like and subscribe for

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.720
more research and videos from AEI.

